{"name":"Canal Blue","hex":"#9cc2c5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"canal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An artificial waterway or artificially improved river used for travel, shipping, or irrigation.","A tubular channel within the body or within a plant."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To dig an artificial waterway in or to (a place), especially for drainage","To travel along a canal by boat"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/canal"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Canal","blocks":[{"text":"Canals or artificial waterways are waterways or engineered channels built for drainage management or for conveyancing water transport vehicles. They carry free, calm surface flow under atmospheric pressure, and can be thought of as artificial rivers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Alter_Strom.jpg/330px-Alter_Strom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canal","wikidata":"Q12284"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/IMG_RoyalCanalnrKinnegad5706w.jpg/500px-IMG_RoyalCanalnrKinnegad5706w.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/IMG_RoyalCanalnrKinnegad5706w.jpg/1280px-IMG_RoyalCanalnrKinnegad5706w.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/IMG_RoyalCanalnrKinnegad5706w.jpg/500px-IMG_RoyalCanalnrKinnegad5706w.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Royal Canal in Ireland","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIMG_RoyalCanalnrKinnegad5706w.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Basingstoke_Canal_-_Lock.JPG/500px-Basingstoke_Canal_-_Lock.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Basingstoke_Canal_-_Lock.JPG/1280px-Basingstoke_Canal_-_Lock.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Basingstoke_Canal_-_Lock.JPG/500px-Basingstoke_Canal_-_Lock.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Small boat canals such as the Basingstoke Canal fuelled the Industrial Revolution in much of Europe and the United States.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABasingstoke_Canal_-_Lock.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb